  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on reweighting outcomes with probability strictly less than 20%, but in this problem, both loss outcomes (25% for A, 20% for B) are at or above the 20% threshold, so no reweighting would occur under the reason. Thus, the reason would not activate any special weighting and would not clearly favor either lottery based on its rule. The individual, however, discusses the lower probability of a larger loss in B, indicating they are comparing the risk of extreme outcomes. While this shows sensitivity to low-probability, high-loss events, they do not mention any adjustment of probabilities or focus exclusively on outcomes below 20%. In fact, they refer to 25% and 20% as relevant chances, treating them as meaningful probabilities without implying special weighting. The individual’s reasoning aligns more with a general aversion to large losses than with the specific probability transformation rule defined by the decision reason. There is no evidence they applied a 10 percentage point increase to any probability, nor did they isolate outcomes below 20%. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual consider low-probability losses, the specific mechanism and conditions of the decision reason are not reflected in the report.",
